# This lesson was laid out for a 40x30 board.
board 40 30

text 11,1.5 "How a neural network learns"
say "Here is the question: how does a pile of numbers learn to be right?"

text 4.5,4.5 "Input"
box 5,7 3,2.5 "x1"
box 5,12 3,2.5 "x2"
box 5,17 3,2.5 "x3"
say "Three numbers go in. Nothing here knows what they mean yet."

text 16.5,2.5 "Hidden"
box 17,5 3,2.5 "h1"
box 17,10 3,2.5 "h2"
box 17,15 3,2.5 "h3"
box 17,20 3,2.5 "h4"
say "This middle row is where the learning lives. Everything else is plumbing."

text 28.5,9.5 "Output"
box 29,12 3.5,2.5 "y"
say "One number comes out, and it starts off being wrong."

stroke 8,8.25 17,6.25
stroke 8,8.25 17,11.25
stroke 8,8.25 17,16.25
stroke 8,8.25 17,21.25
stroke 8,13.25 17,6.25
stroke 8,13.25 17,11.25
stroke 8,13.25 17,16.25
stroke 8,13.25 17,21.25
stroke 8,18.25 17,6.25
stroke 8,18.25 17,11.25
stroke 8,18.25 17,16.25
stroke 8,18.25 17,21.25
say "Everything connects to everything, because nothing yet knows what matters."

stroke 20,6.25 29,13.25
stroke 20,11.25 29,13.25
stroke 20,16.25 29,13.25
stroke 20,21.25 29,13.25
say "Each of those connections carries a weight, and the weights are the only thing that changes."
# --- correction: "Hidden" ran into the title. Clear the band and rewrite both.
erase 18,2.2 3
text 11,1.5 "How a neural network learns"
text 17,3.8 "Hidden"

# --- focus: pick one unit to open up
highlight "h2" "Pick any one of them. They are interchangeable."
say "All four do the identical job, so understanding one is understanding the layer."
# --- repair: that erase clipped the top off h1. Redraw it.
box 17,5 3,2.5 "h1"

# --- the zoom: what one unit actually does
text 1.5,23.3 "Inside h2: multiply, add, squash"
say "Three steps, and none of them is cleverer than arithmetic."

text 3.5,25.2 "x1"
text 3.5,26.6 "x2"
text 3.5,28 "x3"
arrow 5.5,25.2 12.6,26 "w1"
arrow 5.5,26.6 12.6,26.5 "w2"
arrow 5.5,28 12.6,27 "w3"
say "A weight is just how much this unit cares about that input."

box 13,25 4.5,3 "sum"
say "Add them up, and you have one number standing for all three inputs."

arrow 17.5,26.5 21,26.5
box 21,25 5.5,3 "ReLU"
say "The squash is what stops the whole network collapsing into one straight line."

arrow 26.5,26.5 31,26.5
text 31.5,26.5 "h2"
say "That is the entire unit. Repeat it a few million times and you have a model."
# tie the prediction to the output box before we clear anything
arrow 32.6,13.25 33.6,13.25
text 34,11.5 "guess"
text 34,13.25 "0.7"
text 34,15 "truth"
text 34,16.7 "1.0"
say "It guessed 0.7. The truth was 1.0, so it was wrong by a third."

# --- wipe the zoom. One rectangle now that erase takes w,h.
erase 0,22.8 40,8 "Forget the arithmetic. What matters is what happens when it is wrong."
say "Hold on to that, because the next part is where it gets fixed."

text 1.5,24.3 "Learning: compare, then push the error backwards"
box 2,26 5,2.8 "guess 0.7"
arrow 7,27.4 9.5,27.4
box 9.5,26 5,2.8 "true 1.0"
arrow 14.5,27.4 17,27.4
box 17,26 6,2.8 "error 0.3"
say "That gap is the only feedback the network ever gets."

arrow 23,27.4 25.5,27.4
box 25.5,26 9,2.8 "nudge the weights"
say "Every weight that pushed the answer down gets blamed in proportion to how much it pushed."

arrow 28,25.8 21,23.2 "backwards"
say "Blame flows backwards through the mesh. That is the whole of backpropagation."
say "Nothing here understood anything. It just stopped being wrong, slowly."
